  "termRpeTitle": "RPE – Rating of Perceived Exertion",
  "termRpeBody": "How hard a workout or set felt, on a scale from 1 (very easy) to 10 (maximal). 3–4 = easy, you can chat; 6–7 = hard but controlled; 9–10 = all-out.",
  "termRpeTip": "Optional. When you enter it, the plan adapts better – e.g. easy runs that feel too hard lead to a lighter next week.",
  "termRirTitle": "RIR – Reps in Reserve",
  "termRirBody": "How many more reps you could have done at the end of a set. \"2 in the tank\" means you stop two reps before failure.",
  "termRirTip": "The effort chips map to it: Easy ≈ 4+, Just right ≈ 2–3, Hard ≈ 1, Limit = 0. Most sets should end with 1–3 in the tank.",
  "termE1rmTitle": "e1RM – estimated one-rep max",
  "termE1rmBody": "The weight you could probably lift exactly once, calculated from a set with more reps (e.g. 80 kg × 8 ≈ 101 kg). No need to test a real max.",
  "termE1rmTip": "Good for comparing progress across different weights and rep counts.",
  "termVdotTitle": "VDOT – running fitness score",
  "termVdotBody": "A score by coach Jack Daniels derived from your race times. All running paces in your plan (easy, tempo, interval) are calculated from it.",
  "termVdotTip": "+1 VDOT is roughly 1–2 % faster over any distance. It rises with consistent training and is updated when you run faster.",
  "termFtpTitle": "FTP – Functional Threshold Power",
  "termFtpBody": "The power (watts) you can hold on the bike for about one hour. Bike zones such as endurance, sweet spot and threshold are percentages of it.",
  "termFtpTip": "Without a power meter the plan uses heart rate and feel instead.",
  "termCssTitle": "CSS – Critical Swim Speed",
  "termCssBody": "Your threshold pace in the pool per 100 m, calculated from a 400 m and a 200 m time trial. Swim intervals are set relative to it.",
  "termCssTip": "Example: 400 m in 8:00 and 200 m in 3:40 → CSS ≈ 2:10 /100 m.",
  "termSupersetTitle": "Superset",
  "termSupersetBody": "Two (or three) exercises done back to back with only a short switch, then the full rest. The app pairs opposing muscles (e.g. press + row), so each recovers while the other works.",
  "termSupersetTip": "Saves time without losing performance.",
  "termAmrapTitle": "AMRAP – as many reps as possible",
  "termAmrapBody": "On the last set do as many clean reps as you can – but stop one rep before failure. The app uses the result to correct your weights.",
  "termAmrapTip": "Only appears when it helps: for new exercises and when you reach the top of the rep range.",
  "termVolumeTitle": "Volume",
  "termVolumeBody": "Weight × reps of all working sets added up (warm-ups don't count). For bodyweight exercises the lifted share of your body weight counts.",
  "termVolumeTip": "More volume is not automatically better – what matters is progress over weeks.",
  "termPhaseTitle": "Plan phases",
  "termPhaseBody": "Base: build endurance and technique, mostly easy. Build: more intensity. Peak: race-specific sessions. Taper: less volume before the race so you start fresh. Recovery week: a lighter week every 3–5 weeks so your body can adapt.",
  "termPhaseTip": "Recovery and taper weeks feel easy on purpose – don't make up for them.",
  "termZonesTitle": "Intensity zones",
  "termZonesBody": "Training ranges from very easy to maximal, based on pace, heart rate or power. About 80 % of endurance training should be easy – that's what builds your base.",
  "termZonesTip": "Tap a zone in a workout for its exact range.",
